2024-03-27T20:01:28 --------------------------
2024-03-27T20:01:28 |Date: Wed, 28 May 2014 18:00:59 -0700
2024-03-27T20:01:28 |From: Philip Guenther <pguenther@proofpoint.com>
2024-03-27T20:01:28 |To: hackers@openbsd.org
2024-03-27T20:01:28 |Subject: perl: stop using /dev/arandom
2024-03-27T20:01:28 |User-Agent: Alpine 2.11 (BSO 23 2013-08-11)
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |Perl_seed() |seed() is a maybe-public API in libperl (c.f. perlapi(1)) which
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |currently reads a U32 from /dev/arandom. That's not as good as just
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |calling arc4random(), as the device doesn't work in chroots, etc. While
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |it's configurable for various other sources, Perl_seed() doesn't currently
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |have a "just call this other function, dammit" define, so here's the blunt
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |hammer diff to pound arc4random() into it.
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |(Perl_seed() is only used in perl itself as the seed generator if you call
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |perl-level srand() without an argument, and similarly the perl-level
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |rand() without previously seeding it.)
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |Yay/nay?
2024-03-27T20:01:29 |
